                  "fontWeight": "medium"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "y8hPJ",
          "name": "articleContent",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "padding": [
            0,
            56,
            64,
            56
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "7WJS3",
              "name": "leadPara",
              "fill": "#1A1A1A",
              "content": "Typography is more than just selecting fonts. It is the art of arranging type in a way that makes written language legible, readable, and appealing when displayed. The arrangement of type involves selecting typefaces, point sizes, line lengths, line-spacing (leading), and letter-spacing (tracking), and adjusting the space between pairs of letters (kerning).",
              "lineHeight": 1.8,
              "fontFamily": "Inter",
              "fontSize": 18,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "x3gxI",
              "name": "section1Title",
              "fill": "#000000",
              "content": "The Fundamentals",
              "fontFamily": "Playfair Display",
              "fontSize": 32,
              "fontWeight": "normal",
              "fontStyle": "italic"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "fm8JN",
              "name": "para1",
              "fill": "#333333",
              "content": "The term typography is also applied to the style, arrangement, and appearance of the letters, numbers, and symbols created by the process. Type design is a closely related craft, sometimes considered part of typography; most typographers do not design typefaces, and some type designers do not consider themselves typographers.",
              "lineHeight": 1.8,
              "fontFamily": "Inter",
              "fontSize": 16,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "iH0Eh",
              "name": "para2",
              "fill": "#333333",
              "content": "Typography is the work of typesetters (formerly known as compositors), typographers, graphic designers, art directors, manga artists, comic book artists, graffiti artists, and, now, anyone who arranges type for a product. Until the Digital Age, typography was a specialized occupation. Digitization opened up typography to new generations of visual designers and lay users.",
              "lineHeight": 1.8,
              "fontFamily": "Inter",
              "fontSize": 16,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            },
            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "XqfJy",
              "name": "pullQuote",
              "padding": [
                48,
                0
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "Fw5mA",
                  "name": "pullQuoteText",
                  "fill": "#000000",
                  "content": "\"Typography needs to be audible. Typography needs to be felt.\"",
                  "fontFamily": "Playfair Display",
                  "fontSize": 32,
                  "fontWeight": "normal",
                  "fontStyle": "italic"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "JIkH1",
                  "name": "pullQuoteAuthor",
                  "fill": "#888888",
                  "content": "— Helmut Schmid",
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 14,
                  "fontWeight": "normal"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "CLu93",
              "name": "section2Title",
              "fill": "#000000",
              "content": "Modern Typography",
              "fontFamily": "Playfair Display",
              "fontSize": 32,
              "fontWeight": "normal",
              "fontStyle": "italic"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "qNeKl",
              "name": "para3",
              "fill": "#333333",
              "content": "In contemporary design, typography has evolved from purely functional considerations to become a powerful expressive medium. Digital tools have expanded the possibilities, allowing designers to experiment with variable fonts, responsive typography, and dynamic layouts that adapt to different screens and contexts.",
              "lineHeight": 1.8,
              "fontFamily": "Inter",
              "fontSize": 16,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "Duc94",
              "name": "para4",
              "fill": "#333333",
              "content": "The choice of typeface and its arrangement on the page can significantly influence how a message is perceived. Bold, sans-serif fonts might convey modernity and strength, while elegant serif fonts can suggest tradition and sophistication. The weight, size, and spacing of type all contribute to the overall visual hierarchy and readability of the content.",
              "lineHeight": 1.8,
              "fontFamily": "Inter",
              "fontSize": 16,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            },
            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "lWSmc",
              "name": "keyPointsBox",
              "padding": 32,
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "n9in9",
                  "name": "keyPointsTitle",
                  "fill": "#000000",
                  "content": "Key Takeaways",
                  "fontFamily": "Playfair Display",
                  "fontSize": 20,
                  "fontWeight": "semibold",
                  "fontStyle": "italic"
                },
                {
                  "type": "frame",
                  "id": "h15rI",
                  "name": "keyPoint1",
                  "gap": 12,
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "tGEEC",
                      "name": "keyPoint1Bullet",
                      "fill": "#000000",
                      "content": "•",
                      "fontFamily": "Inter",
                      "fontSize": 16,
                      "fontWeight": "bold"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "ebyC4",
                      "name": "keyPoint1Text",
                      "fill": "#333333",
                      "content": "Typography combines art and technique to make written language legible and appealing",
                      "fontFamily": "Inter",
                      "fontSize": 14,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "frame",
                  "id": "83jfH",
                  "name": "keyPoint2",
                  "gap": 12,
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "Bgv3V",
                      "name": "keyPoint2Bullet",
                      "fill": "#000000",
                      "content": "•",
                      "fontFamily": "Inter",
                      "fontSize": 16,
                      "fontWeight": "bold"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "8eMYr",
                      "name": "keyPoint2Text",
                      "fill": "#333333",
                      "content": "Digital tools have revolutionized typographic possibilities",
                      "fontFamily": "Inter",
                      "fontSize": 14,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "frame",
                  "id": "XHefh",
                  "name": "keyPoint3",
                  "gap": 12,
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "RzybE",
                      "name": "keyPoint3Bullet",
                      "fill": "#000000",
                      "content": "•",
                      "fontFamily": "Inter",
                      "fontSize": 16,
                      "fontWeight": "bold"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "2Ulsn",
                      "name": "keyPoint3Text",
                      "fill": "#333333",
                      "content": "Typeface selection and arrangement significantly influence message perception",
                      "fontFamily": "Inter",
                      "fontSize": 14,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "I0xCM",
              "name": "section3Title",
              "fill": "#000000",
              "content": "Conclusion",
              "fontFamily": "Playfair Display",
              "fontSize": 32,
              "fontWeight": "normal",
              "fontStyle": "italic"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "DqjN3",
              "name": "para5",
              "fill": "#333333",
              "content": "Whether in print or on screen, typography remains a fundamental aspect of design that bridges the gap between content and comprehension. As we continue to innovate and explore new possibilities, the core principles of typography—clarity, hierarchy, and expression—remain as relevant today as they have ever been.",
              "lineHeight": 1.8,
              "fontFamily": "Inter",
              "fontSize": 16,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
